---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import { LINKS } from '@/lib/links';

import { LAST_UPDATED, formatLastUpdated } from '@/lib/page-dates.mjs';

const lastUpdated = formatLastUpdated(LAST_UPDATED.corpus);

// Rendered as the visible link text too. Derived rather than retyped so the URL
// cannot drift from `LINKS.corpus`: a link whose label disagrees with its href
// is exactly the failure links.ts exists to prevent.
const corpusLinkText = LINKS.corpus.replace(/^https:\/\//, '');

// The tiles below paint with `bg-brand-light-gray` / `text-brand-gray` rather
// than the `bg-card` / `text-muted-foreground` semantic utilities. Those resolve
// through `hsl(var(--card))`, but globals.css defines `--card` as a hex
// (`theme('colors.brand.light-gray')`), so the browser sees `hsl(#f6f6f6)`,
// discards it as invalid, and paints nothing. Fixing those tokens site-wide is
// its own change; these tiles just avoid depending on them.
//
// Every figure below is the count that actually SHIPS in the published
// parquet, taken from the export's own reconciliation ledger
// (`reconciliation.json`, alongside the data on the Hub) rather than from the
// source database. The two differ: the database holds 12,271 options and
// 5,286 endorsements, of which 4 and 3 respectively are quarantined by the
// export gates and never published. Quoting the database numbers would
// advertise rows a downloader cannot find.
const FIGURES = [
  { value: '230', label: 'concepts' },
  { value: '12,267', label: 'options' },
  { value: '5,283', label: 'endorsements' },
  { value: '76', label: 'contributors' },
  { value: '56', label: 'locales' },
];

// The four obligation-mapped source values. The wording of `meaning` and `owed`
// is held word-for-word with the dataset card's own table (CORE-1720): the site
// and the card describe one taxonomy, and a paraphrase here would read as a
// second, subtly different promise.
const SOURCES = [
  {
    value: 'legesher',
    meaning:
      'Produced by the Legesher project: our own packs, curated corrections, values we composed from existing pack material',
    owed: 'nothing beyond this licence',
  },
  {
    value: 'upstream',
    meaning: 'Lifted from another open-source project',
    owed: "that project's notice and credit, below",
  },
  {
    value: 'community',
    meaning: 'Offered or attested by an individual through our native-review process',
    owed: 'credit on request, withdrawal on request',
  },
  {
    value: 'model',
    meaning: 'Generated by a language model',
    owed: 'disclosure of which model, below',
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="The Language Corpus - Legesher"
  description="An open corpus of programming vocabulary in human languages: every word anyone has offered, every endorsement it received, and where each word came from."
>
  <Header />
  <main class="pt-32 pb-20 bg-white">
    <div class="container mx-auto px-4 max-w-4xl">
      <header class="mb-12">
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold mb-4">The language corpus</h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-muted-foreground mb-2">
          Every word anyone has offered for a programming concept in a human language, and every endorsement a
          person gave it. Open data, with the source of every word recorded.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
          <span>Last updated: {lastUpdated}</span>
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-5 gap-4 mb-16">
        {FIGURES.map((figure) => (
          <div class="bg-brand-light-gray border border-brand-gray/30 p-4 text-center">
            <div class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-brand-cyan">{figure.value}</div>
            <div class="text-sm text-brand-gray">{figure.label}</div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <article class="prose prose-lg max-w-none prose-headings:text-foreground prose-a:text-brand-cyan hover:prose-a:text-brand-coral prose-table:text-sm">
        <section aria-labelledby="what-it-is">
          <h2 id="what-it-is">What the corpus holds</h2>
          <p>
            The corpus holds every candidate word (an &ldquo;option&rdquo;) that anyone has offered for a
            programming-language concept in a human language. Offers come from Legesher, from other open-source
            projects, from community reviewers and from language models. Each option carries the endorsements people
            gave it.
          </p>
          <p>
            The question &ldquo;what is <code>if</code> in Telugu?&rdquo; has more than one answer. People offer
            different words at different times for different reasons, and the disagreement is part of the record.
            The corpus keeps every answer, with the evidence beside it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Each option has a stable identifier derived from its content, so a rebuild or a re-export produces the
            same corpus byte for byte. Nothing is deleted. A word that was later replaced stays in the corpus next to
            its replacement.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="canon-split">
          <h2 id="canon-split">What is published separately</h2>
          <p>
            The corpus is the record of what was offered. The <strong>canon</strong>, the word Legesher ships for
            each concept in each language, is a separate dataset,
            <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/legesher/language-canon" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >language-canon</a
            >, and the language packs are built from it. In the words of the canon&rsquo;s own card, the corpus is the
            full record of what has been suggested; the canon is the vocabulary that downstream applications actually
            use.
          </p>
          <p>
            The corpus does not say which word is right. It records what was offered and what was endorsed. The
            private link from a contributor identifier back to a person is never published either; the export stops
            rather than include it.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="provenance">
          <h2 id="provenance">Where each word came from</h2>
          <p>
            Every row carries a <code>sources</code> list naming the kinds of source that offered or attested the
            word. There are four values, and each one answers the same question: what do we owe for this word?
          </p>
          {/* The only wide element on the page. Scrolling it inside its own box
              keeps a narrow screen from having to scroll the whole document
              sideways to read one cell. */}
          <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="min-w-[34rem]">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th><code>sources</code> value</th>
                <th>What it means</th>
                <th>What we owe</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {SOURCES.map((source) => (
                <tr>
                  <td><code>{source.value}</code></td>
                  <td>{source.meaning}</td>
                  <td>{source.owed}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            An option can carry more than one value. A word proposed by a model and later attested by a reviewer
            carries both. Counts per value are not fixed in the dataset card; they can be computed from the data, as
            endorsement counts can.
          </p>
          <p>
            Finer-grained provenance is not published. That field carries reviewer labels that would identify
            people, and nothing in the corpus leads from a contributor identifier back to a person. For the same
            reason the upstream project names are in the dataset card, not in a column.
          </p>
          <p>
            <code>legesher</code> says who produced a word, not how good it is. A word Legesher produced has the
            standing its endorsements give it, and no more. A word a model generated is a starting point. Packs built
            from words no person has attested are labeled experimental.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="licence">
          <h2 id="licence">License and attribution</h2>
          <p>
            The corpus is released under <strong>CC BY 4.0</strong>. You may share and adapt it, including
            commercially, as long as you give credit.
          </p>
          <p>
            Part of the corpus comes from other open-source projects with their own terms. Each project&rsquo;s
            license is linked from the dataset card, and the notices they ask us to keep travel with the data in its
            <code>NOTICE</code> file. CC BY 4.0 covers the <strong>compilation</strong>; it does not relicense the
            contributed parts.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <a href="https://github.com/legesher/language-packs" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
                >legesher-translations</a
              > (now <code>language-packs</code>, MIT). 149 contributors are credited in that repository&rsquo;s
              <a
                href="https://github.com/legesher/language-packs/blob/main/.all-contributorsrc"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer">.all-contributorsrc</a
              >, which remains the roster of record. The repository is Legesher&rsquo;s own, and the credit is a
              commitment we keep to those 149 people.
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href="https://github.com/UniversalPython/UniversalPython" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
                >UniversalPython</a
              > (Apache License 2.0), Urdu words. <strong>Changes were made.</strong> These words were adapted to our
              identifier conventions and re-keyed to our concept slugs, and some have since been replaced by other
              options in the corpus. For UniversalPython&rsquo;s current form of a word, consult the upstream project.
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href="https://github.com/lenguaje-latino/latino" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Latino</a>
              (MIT), Spanish words.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Words marked <code>model</code> were produced by a language model:
            <a href="https://huggingface.co/CohereLabs/aya-101" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Aya 101</a>
            (Cohere For AI, Apache-2.0) or Claude (Anthropic; outputs owned by the API customer). Where a run recorded
            the model version, the source database keeps it. The earliest machine-generated rows predate version
            recording, so the card names the model family rather than a version per row.
          </p>
          <p>
            Community-contributed translation data is never sold. It is not part of the datasets we license
            commercially. See <a href="/terms#licensing-model">our licensing model</a>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="withdrawal">
          <h2 id="withdrawal">If you contributed</h2>
          <p>
            In the corpus you appear as an opaque, salted identifier, never as a name, handle or email address. The
            link back to you is held privately and is never included in a published copy.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Credit is opt-in.</strong> You are anonymous by default, and your contribution counts the same
            either way. At any time you can ask to be credited by name, to change how you are credited, or to be
            removed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Withdrawal removes you and keeps the work.</strong> We redact every column that identifies you
            and record that a withdrawal happened. The words you offered and the endorsements you gave stay as
            anonymous records, because other contributors&rsquo; work depends on them, and nothing connects them to
            you. No published copy has ever carried an identity, so a withdrawal never requires a release to be
            retracted.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Two limits.</strong> If you submitted through a public pull request or issue, that submission is
            in a public repository under your own account, and we cannot unpublish what you published. Where your
            details were recorded in our own repository, removal applies going forward; earlier revision history is
            cleared separately, not on request. Ask us and we will tell you where that stands.
          </p>
          <p>
            To be credited, to change how you are credited, or to withdraw, email
            <a href="mailto:privacy@legesher.com">privacy@legesher.com</a>. The full contributor notice is
            <a
              href="https://github.com/legesher/language-packs/blob/main/CONTRIBUTOR_PRIVACY.md"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"><code>CONTRIBUTOR_PRIVACY.md</code></a
            > in the language-packs repository.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="get-the-data">
          <h2 id="get-the-data">Get the data</h2>
          <p>
            The corpus is on Hugging Face as five configs: <code>concepts</code>, <code>terms</code>,
            <code>options</code>, <code>endorsements</code> and <code>contributors</code>. The upstream notices, the
            privacy notice and a reconciliation ledger are published with the data.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href={LINKS.corpus} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="break-words">{corpusLinkText}</a>
          </p>
          <p>
            If you speak one of these languages and a word looks wrong to you, tell us.
            <a href={LINKS.slack} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Find us in Slack</a> or open an issue on
            <a href={LINKS.github} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a>.
          </p>
        </section>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
